WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.983
The carriage stopped in front of a modest-looking courthouse.

2
00:00:03.984 --> 00:00:09.402
Mr. Fogg, Passepartout, and Aouda were taken into a room with barred windows.

3
00:00:09.403 --> 00:00:15.642
"You will appear before Judge Obadiah at half past eight," said the policeman.

4
00:00:15.643 --> 00:00:23.642
After the policeman had left them and closed the door, Passepartout fell into a chair and exclaimed, "Why are we prisoners?"

5
00:00:23.643 --> 00:00:30.234
Trying to conceal her emotions, Aouda, said to Mr. Fogg, "Sir, you must leave me to my fate!

6
00:00:30.235 --> 00:00:34.199
You have been arrested because of me—because you saved me!"

7
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:38.200
Phileas Fogg explained that this was impossible.

8
00:00:38.201 --> 00:00:43.439
It was unlikely that he would be arrested for having prevented a human sacrifice.

9
00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.840
He would not, in any event, abandon Aouda but would escort her to Hong Kong.

10
00:00:48.841 --> 00:00:54.257
"But the steamer leaves at noon!" observed Passepartout nervously.

11
00:00:54.258 --> 00:00:58.494
"And we shall be on board by noon," replied Fogg.

12
00:00:58.495 --> 00:01:04.024
He said it so positively that Passepartout couldn't help but be reassured.

13
00:01:04.025 --> 00:01:07.035
At half past eight, the door opened.

14
00:01:07.036 --> 00:01:10.952
The policeman reappeared and instructed them to follow. He

15
00:01:10.953 --> 00:01:19.320
led them to a courtroom. Mr. Fogg and his two companions entered and took their places at a desk opposite the judge. Passepartout

16
00:01:19.321 --> 00:01:28.482
was getting more nervous. On the clock over the judge's head, the hands seemed to be going around very, very quickly. Judge

17
00:01:28.483 --> 00:01:34.164
Obadiah was a fat, round man who wore an old, white wig. "The

18
00:01:34.165 --> 00:01:39.364
first case, Mr. Phileas Fogg," he said. "I

19
00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:42.197
am here," replied Mr. Fogg. "Passepartout?"

20
00:01:42.198 --> 00:01:45.118
asked the judge. "Present,"

21
00:01:45.119 --> 00:01:48.139
responded Passepartout. "Good,"

22
00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:56.068
said the judge. "Authorities have been searching for you for two days on the train from Bombay." A

23
00:01:56.069 --> 00:02:00.817
door swung open, and three Indian priests entered. The

24
00:02:00.818 --> 00:02:06.512
priests took their places in front of the judge, and the clerk of the court stood. In

25
00:02:06.513 --> 00:02:11.328
a loud voice, he read a complaint of sacrilege against Phileas Fogg and his servant, who

26
00:02:11.329 --> 00:02:17.499
were accused of having violated a place held sacred by the Brahmin religion. "You

27
00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.910
hear the charge?" asked the judge. "Yes,

28
00:02:20.911 --> 00:02:26.538
sir," replied Mr. Fogg, consulting his watch, "and I admit to it." "You

29
00:02:26.539 --> 00:02:29.325
admit it?" asked the judge. "Yes,"

30
00:02:29.326 --> 00:02:37.551
said Fogg. "And I wish to hear these priests admit to what they were going to do at the pagoda." The

31
00:02:37.552 --> 00:02:42.650
priests looked at each other, not seeming to understand what he was saying. "At

32
00:02:42.651 --> 00:02:49.440
the pagoda of Pillaji," said Passepartout. "They were on the point of burning their victim." The

33
00:02:49.441 --> 00:02:54.198
judge stared with astonishment, and the priests were shocked. "What

34
00:02:54.199 --> 00:03:02.348
victim?" asked the judge. "We are talking about the pagoda on Malabar Hill, in Bombay." The

35
00:03:02.349 --> 00:03:06.093
judge placed a pair of shoes on his desk. "My

36
00:03:06.094 --> 00:03:10.535
shoes!" cried Passepartout in surprise. In

37
00:03:10.536 --> 00:03:18.275
all the confusion since they had left Bombay, Phileas Fogg and his servant had quite forgotten the affair over Passepartout's shoes. They

38
00:03:18.276 --> 00:03:24.419
also did not notice the detective, Fix, watching from a corner of the courtroom. Fix

39
00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:29.939
had foreseen how Passepartout's misadventure could detain Mr. Fogg in Calcutta. He had

40
00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:38.375
gone to talk with the priests on Malabar Hill, promised them a large sum of money, and sent them to Calcutta on the next train. The priests

41
00:03:38.376 --> 00:03:45.770
had arrived ahead of Mr. Fogg and Passepartout, however, and Fix had been feverishly watching the train station in Calcutta. When Fix

42
00:03:45.771 --> 00:03:53.019
had seen Mr. Fogg and Passepartout disembark that morning, he had immediately found a policeman to arrest the men. Judge Obadiah

43
00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:59.399
had heard Passepartout's outburst about his shoes and said, "The facts are admitted. I condemn

44
00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:06.783
this man, Passepartout, to imprisonment for fifteen days and a fine of three hundred pounds. I further

45
00:04:06.784 --> 00:04:14.610
condemn Mr. Fogg to a week's imprisonment and a fine of one hundred and fifty pounds." Fix rubbed

46
00:04:14.611 --> 00:04:17.667
his hands with satisfaction. If Phileas

47
00:04:17.668 --> 00:04:25.354
Fogg could be detained in Calcutta for a week, it would be enough time for the arrest warrant to arrive from London. Phileas Fogg,

48
00:04:25.355 --> 00:04:30.553
as self-composed as ever, said, "I offer bail." Fix's blood

49
00:04:30.554 --> 00:04:33.188
ran cold. "You have

50
00:04:33.189 --> 00:04:37.102
that right," said the judge. "I will

51
00:04:37.103 --> 00:04:43.175
pay it at once," said Mr. Fogg, taking a roll of banknotes from the carpetbag. Bail was

52
00:04:43.176 --> 00:04:48.223
set at two thousand pounds, and Mr. Fogg handed the money to the clerk. "This sum

53
00:04:48.224 --> 00:04:58.079
will be restored to you at the end of your sentence," said the judge. "Meanwhile you are liberated on bail." "Come!" said

54
00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:01.622
Phileas Fogg to his servant. He offered

55
00:05:01.623 --> 00:05:04.999
his arm to Aouda and they departed. Fix followed

56
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:13.452
them to the quay. His only hope was that Fogg would not be willing to leave the two thousand pounds behind him. The steamer

57
00:05:13.453 --> 00:05:17.744
departing for Hong Kong was the Rangoon . A

58
00:05:17.745 --> 00:05:22.287
flag flying from its mast signaled that it was about to depart. Fix

59
00:05:22.288 --> 00:05:32.280
watched as Fogg and his companions got out of their carriage and pushed off in a boat for the steamer. Fix stamped his feet in disappointment. "The rascal

60
00:05:32.281 --> 00:05:38.872
is off after all!" he said. "Two thousand pounds sacrificed! I'll follow

61
00:05:38.873 --> 00:05:42.487
him to the end of the world if necessary. But at

62
00:05:42.488 --> 00:05:50.561
the rate Fogg is going, the money he stole will soon be exhausted!" Passepartout's Guidebook

63
00:05:50.562 --> 00:05:55.764
That was a close one! Thank goodness Mr. Fogg was able to post bail.

64
00:05:55.765 --> 00:06:01.868
But I must be careful. My mistakes are costing him so much money!

65
00:06:01.869 --> 00:06:07.661
I couldn't help noticing that Judge Obadiah's wig and clothes seemed royal, like something a king would wear.

66
00:06:07.662 --> 00:06:13.626
Interestingly, I learned that this costume does have links to royalty—French royalty!

67
00:06:13.627 --> 00:06:18.942
In the sixteenth century, King Louis XIII, of France, began wearing a wig.

68
00:06:18.943 --> 00:06:23.375
It didn't take long for all wealthy aristocrats to start wearing wigs too.

69
00:06:23.376 --> 00:06:28.252
However, by the eighteenth century, wigs became less and less popular.

70
00:06:28.253 --> 00:06:32.646
Today in England lawyers and judges are the only people who still wear them.

71
00:06:32.647 --> 00:06:39.646
And that's fine with me. I think I look perfect like this anyway!

